<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Lordship of the Isles</h1><p class="st-meta">1336 CE – 1493 CE · capital Finlaggan (Islay)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The MacDonald sea lordship of the Hebrides and western Highlands, a Gaelic power so strong its lords negotiated with England as near-independent princes.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-islay">John of Islay</a>, a descendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somerled">Somerled</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-argyll-and-the-isles">Kingdom of Argyll and the Isles</a>, styled himself <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-of-the-isles">Dominus Insularum</a>, Lord of the Isles, from 1336. Through marriage and royal favour he united most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrides">Hebrides</a> and much of the western mainland under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clan-donald">Clan Donald</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The lords held council at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finlaggan">Finlaggan</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islay">Islay</a>, with a council of the isles, and patronised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iona">Iona</a> abbey, galley-building, Gaelic poets and physicians. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donald-lord-of-the-isles">Donald of the Isles</a> fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-harlaw">Battle of Harlaw</a> in 1411 over the earldom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ross-scotland">Ross</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-islay-earl-of-ross">John MacDonald II's</a> secret <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-westminster-ardtornish">Treaty of Westminster-Ardtornish</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-iv-of-england">Edward IV</a> in 1462 planned to partition <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When the treaty was discovered, John lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ross-scotland">Ross</a> in 1475. In 1493 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-iv-of-scotland">James IV</a> forfeited the lordship, and the title was later annexed to the heir of the Scottish crown in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-scotland">Kingdom of Scotland</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/lordship-of-the-isles.jpg" alt="Lordship of the Isles" width="320" height="374"><figcaption>Image: Sodacan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arms_of_the_Lord_of_the_Isles.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1336 CE – 1493 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Finlaggan (Islay)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>John of Islay, Donald of the Isles, John MacDonald II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gaelic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Rioghachd nan Eilean, Lords of the Isles</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1414&amp;focus=lordship-of-the-isles" aria-label="Lordship of the Isles on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4656 338 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lordship of the Isles lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4897 579h0M4892 500h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4976 594h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M4976 594h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day United Kingdom. <a href="/atlas?year=1414&amp;focus=lordship-of-the-isles">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
